{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>When a man gives to his fellow a donkey or an ox.</b> The first passage was stated about an unpaid bailee — therefore he exempted him for theft, as stated (verse 6): ״וְגֻנַּב מִבֵּית הָאִישׁ, אִם לֹא יִמָּצֵא הַגַּנָּב״ — and the owner of the house draws near to swear; you have learned that he exempts himself with this oath. And this passage is stated about a paid bailee — therefore he is not exempt if it was stolen, as written (verse 11): ״אִם גָּנֹב יִגָּנֵב מֵעִמּוֹ יְשַׁלֵּם״; but for unavoidable accident — such as it died on its own, or broke, or was captured by force at the hands of bandits — there is no witness to testify about the matter."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:52.036914713Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}